    <File_Area_Ancillary>
        <File>
            <file_name>arrokoth_porter_2024_v01.obj</file_name>
            <creation_date_time>2024-06-19</creation_date_time>
            <file_size unit="byte">3686921</file_size>
            <md5_checksum>26ebfb9fe83b01b2b222534dd0bc5ff5</md5_checksum>
            <comment>The shape model has the following internal format:

                g Arrokoth
                v          x1          y1          z1
                v          x2          y2          z2
                v          x3          y3          z3
                (etc.)
                vt          u1          v1
                vt          u2          v2
                vt          u3          v3
                (etc.)
                vn          x1          y1          z1
                vn          x2          y2          z2
                vn          x3          y3          z3
                (etc.)
                f      i1/it1      j1/jt1      k1/kt1
                f      i2/it2      j2/jt2      k2/kt2
                f      i3/it3      j3/jt3      k3/kt3
                (etc.)

                where the letter 'g' denotes an object name, 'v' denotes a
                vertex, 'vt' denotes a texture, 'vn' denotes a vertex normal,
                and 'f' denotes a triangular face. Texture coordinates refer
                to the provided albedo map.

                A comment line starting with # is provided at the beginning
                of each section.

                The high precision of the vertex, texture, and normal values
                do not imply a similar precision of the shape model. Also,
                the two lobes of the shape model slightly overlap where they
                connect, which affects volume calculations. Please see Porter,
                et al (2024b) for a discussion of uncertainties in the shape
                model.
            </comment>
        </File>
        <Header>
            <offset unit="byte">0</offset>
            <object_length unit="byte">42</object_length>
